A||||7||Philphine||welding magnesium?||||1||
Z||000000||Philphine||03-28-2001||07:40 PM||||just looking at the black gt in the daytona pics, in paticular the water drainage ductwork and wondering how that could be cleaned up a bit.kind of like a personal in my head styling exercise. but im wondering if the gt vents are made of magnesium like the '84's were or aluminum. can you weld it like aluminum, and more specifically could you weld aluminum to magnesium? just want the info to keep in my head if the option comes up some day.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||85Josh||03-28-2001||08:24 PM||||Philphine, this stuff catches fire! I'm a machinist by trade, and once saw a teammate set our lathe on fire by cutting a little too fast. I'm sure someone can weld it, but sure isn't for the amature<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4<BR>CAVALRY SCOUT!(stuck in Field Artillery)||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroJoe||03-28-2001||10:55 PM||||When you light Mg on fire with an ordinary lighter, it burns about as bright as an arc weld.  So, no, you probably can't weld it.  Welding aluminum can be done, though the way I understand it it requires some expensive equipment.  I once tried to braze Al, but it didnt work out to well.<P>I belive Mg is most commonly cast, like wheels and the 84 deck vents were probably a casting.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Standard||03-28-2001||11:32 PM||||The GT vents (and everything else after 84) are cast aluminum, I think.  Definatly not magnesium.  The 84 vents aren't solid magnesium, they're an alloy that contains magnesium.  You'd have to grind it into a fine powder before it becomes a hazard. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||03-28-2001||11:39 PM||||They're not cast. They're stamped and assembled.||||reg||1
Z||000005||baldlobo||03-29-2001||12:10 AM||||even if it's an alloy you would have to do the welding in an inert(sorry about spelling if it's wrong)gas environment.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Wolfhound||03-29-2001||06:00 AM||||Those magnesium deck vents are probably responsible for the extra press 84 Fiero engine fires recieved. Had to be a sight to behold. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||BENGAL4||03-29-2001||01:51 PM||||You can weld magnesium but only in the TiG or MiG process. You would either use AZ62A or AZ61A filler metal. Argon would be the shielding gas. IMHO, I would stick to aluminum. It is easier to work with and not as expensive. Finally, you cannot join alum to mag in the weld process. <P>Bill<BR>86GT 3800 II S/C ||||reg||1
